Principal highways: Amboy Rd., Hyland Ave., Main St. Many foreclosures in this section. Old native element still dominating. Most of the residents were employed in factories across the river in New Jersey with the partial shut down of many of these. Stagnation is apparent in Tottenville. Supplemental Information: Sales Prices (Dec.1940): Prices on properties which were quoted at $7000 in 1938-39 have dropped about 10% or more. Approaching the bottom of the range for singles, the price drop becomes progressively less, 5% being the average loss in value for the smaller houses in the $2500 class. Two family structures are at an absolute minimum of demand and few indeed are sales of this type. Prices would be arbitrary and are not even suggested, as sellers will take almost anything they can get in forced sales of this kind. Rental Prices (Dec.1940): Rents were low here in 1939 and have not regained any of the drop which followed the industrial decline in Tottenville about 1930. The village is too far removed from the New York ferry and there are no local opportunities for employment. Transportation via the Outerbridge Crossing to nearby New Jersey is expensive, and those who are employed in Jersey factories prefer to live on that side of the river (Arthur Kill). Prices today would be no different than they were two years ago, i.e. $22 to $45 for singles; $25 to $45 for two family units. General Comments: Attractions are away from Tottenville rather than to it. Some houses are standing vacant because it is difficult to find tenants because of the distance to the New York ferry, and the cost of getting there (25 cents). This was a fairly thriving town when the shipyards were operating, but with their closing in 1930 came a decline. There is very little activity here today. HOLC holds some 36 houses unsold and institutions have an estimated 75 more. As stated, sales have been very few. HOLC sold only one during 1940. Here again, great activity would probably result from the approval of the proposed Prince's Bay drydock site, which is now under consideration. Typical Sales: |Address|Type|Price&Terms|Remarks| (
) |395 Slate Avenue| 6 room frame single.| $3500-$500 down with lease and option; 5% interest.| Sold by Westerleigh S&L at a net loss of $1020. No reconditioning.| (
) |69 Summit Road| 6 room 2 story single; steam heat; garage.| $4500-no down payment (tenant bought it; had been in 5 years); 5% interest lease and option.| Sold by Richmond County Federal S&L in fair condition; no reconditioning| (
) | 119 Barnard Avenue| 6 room single.| $3250-$325 down.|Reconditioned.| (
) |6 Hecker Street| 6 room single.| $2500-$250 down.| Reconditioned.| (
) |8 Hecker Street| 6 room single.| $2500-$250 down.| Reconditioned.| (
) |10 Satterlee Street| 5 room single with garage.| $4000-$400 down.| Reconditioned.|

Principal highway: Amboy Rd. These are 3 of the oldest villages on Staten Island. Very little change is noted here, with the old native element still in the majority. Supplemental Information: Sales Prices (Dec. 1940): A negligible sales demand and consequent lack of activity have resulted in little or no change in prices for single houses, of which the area is entirely comprised. Today's range approximates that of 1939: $3500 to $5500. A few small new cottages have been sold in here for prices ranging in $2500 class, but these are exceptional and not at all consistent with the balance of the area. Rental Prices (Dec. 1940): Same as for 1938-39; singles renting for $30 to $45 and not much activity very little seasonal movement in this district is too rural in character. Very few vacancies observed in a tour of the area. General Comments: Some new construction in here recently: 14 small 4 to 5 room bungalows just south of Amboy Road in the small detached section of C-23. These were built by a Brooklyn concern and trouble was experienced even before completion, with the sanitation. The land is low at that point and drainage is insufficient. The condition was so bad that the Sanitary Department refused to issue certificates of occupancy for these houses. They were sold at various prices with this defect, probably $2500 being the top price, and are now all occupied. In general the area is best north of Amboy Road, though even here poor houses are scattered among the better ones. The three "stems" extending northward in this area are all very sparsely developed, perhaps about 20 to 30%. Much of the area was cut through with streets and sidewalks years ago in the hope of future growth which never came. This is particularly true of the eastern most "finger", along Hugenot Avenue, where there are only two streets with houses: Rathbun and Rensselaer (north of Edgegrove). The street surface in here is very bad on all side streets: sidewalks are partially overgrown with small trees and brush. Three new bungalows with built in garages on Ramona Avenue, a brand new dual highway which opens up some good building sites in this area. HOLC has 15 houses in the area; institutions have an estimated 50 at the most. Sales have been rarae. (None for HOLC). Approval of the new dry dock site in Prince's Bay should make a tremendous difference here. Typical Sales (
) |Address | Type| Price & Terms| Remarks| (
) |27 Spooner Street (extreme east end of area)| 2 story frame and stucco: 8 rooms.| $6500-$500 down; Contract: 5% interest.| Sold by Richmond County Federal S&L with $700 reconditioning| (
) |810 Marcey Avenue| 5 room bungalow, unfinished upstairs; 1 car garage.| $3700-$250 down: Contract: 5% interest| Sold by Richmond County Federal S&L without reconditioning. Was on books for $3440.| (
).

Principal highways: Hylan Blvd., Richmond Rd. There were many foreclosures here. As a general rule the 2-family units were better built than the singles. Supplemental Information: Sales Prices (Dec.1940): Higher priced singles have come down sharply where any sales at all are noted; the house quoted at $7000 in 1938 may be offered at $5500 today. Two family structures have suffered even more; whereas $8500 to $12,000 was mentioned as a fair bracket in 1938, today it would be more like $6000 to $8500, with no demand whatever at the second figure. Rental Prices (Dec.1940): Rents were always pretty low in here, considering property values, and therefore haven't changed noticeably within the past two years. Singles still bring from $20 to $45; apartments in two family dwellings from $40 to $45 heated. Demand is good and there appear to be very few vacancies. General Comments: HOLC has upwards of 40 houses in this area. Many were early foreclosures and have not been reappraised, consequently are priced somewhat higher than competing houses. Few offers are made on property in this area. Property management brokers operating in this area cite instances where the Corporation refused offers two months ago which they are now seeking to revive. Competitors are not selling well in here either; although they have a heavy load at present. Majority of sales are to Italians, who buy sacrifice property in wife's name. No new construction has gone in during the past year and the area is noticeably "stagnant", probably because of the racial factor. Institutions have made consecutive reductions of their offerings without apparently having much effect on speeding up liquidation. No future improvement forecast and the trend is downward. Typical Sales: |Address|Type|Price&Terms|Remarks| (
) |76 Seaview Avenue| 2 family frame dwelling| $2500-10% down; Lease and option for 5 years, mortgage at end of term, written for 3 years at 5% interest; month amortization.| Sold by Staten Is. Savings Bank "as is" (rentable).| (
) |233 Buel Avenue| Frame single with brick trim, good condition.| $3900-Cash| Sold by Title Guaranty and Trust Company.| (
) |293 Buel Avenue| 6 room stucco on frame; 2 car garage; oil burner.| $4200-Cash| Sold by Title Guaranty and Trust Company.| (
) |66-69 Parkinson Avenue| Duplex house| $6500-$350 down; Lease and option; 5 years; 5% interest| Sold by Westerleigh S&L Assn. at $985 net loss. Purchaser converted to 4 family.| (
)

*Funds available only for lending in southerly extremity. Principal highways: Bay St., Va Duzon St. Large portion of area is unrestricted with a high ratio of assessed valuation to market value in those parts. Supplemental Information: Sales Prices (Dec.1940): Weaker on all classes of housing than in 1938-39. Singles are priced generally from $2000 to $6500, with little demand toward the upper end of the bracket. Most sales reported are around $4000. Two family structures are in little demand and due to institutional price cutting in the effort to reduce inventories, have come down to something like $3500 to $7500, which may be termed an arbitrary bracket inasmuch as there have been no sales reported recently. A few old three and four family dwellings, "old law tenements" are scattered throughout this area. These have been in no demand, and prices are arbitrarily mentioned as $3000 to $5500, a little lower than in 1938-39. Rental Prices (Dec. 1940): No appreciable change in rents. Singles range from $20 to $45- most of them nearer the former figure: two family units at $20 to $50, the latter figure being for those where heat is supplied; flats in the old law tenements, three and four family, fall within $15 to $25, some with heat, most of them unheated. General Comments: This section was expected to improve with the coming of the Free Port immediately adjacent on the east. However, these expectations were not realized, possibly due to stringency of manufacturing laws and to other restrictions imposed. There has been very little development here accordingly, and residential districts adjoining have derived no benefits. Until recently the Zone was used for loading planes and trucks for shipment to France, but with the collapse of the French Republic this activity has ceased and the piers are for the most part idle. Best residential values and prospects are along the thoroughfare separating the area from the Fox Hills Golf Club property on the south (Vanderbilt Avenue). HOLC has 12 parcels in the area at present and has sold 5 since last May. No new construction was noted during a tour of the area, and the trend is at best static and possibly downward slightly. Typical Sales: |Address|Type| Price&Terms| Remarks| (
) |223 Van Duzer St.| 8 room frame single| $4200-10% down; lease and option: 5 year term; interest 5%.| Sold by Staten Island Savings Bank after painting only.| (
) |34 Quince Street| Small frame single| $1300-$150 down; lease with option; interest at 5%.| Sold by Westerleigh S&L Association without reconditioning, at net fain of $250.(
) | 7 Bowen Street| 6 room frame single, with garage.| $4000 cash| Sold by Title Guaranty and Trust Company, all in good "rentable" condition due to minor reconditioning. These had been renting from $30 to $40.| (
) 9 Nunnally Court| 6 room frame single| $4000 cash| Sold by Title Guaranty and Trust Company, all in good "rentable" condition due to minor reconditioning. These had been renting from $30 to $40.| (
) |11 Nunnally Court| 6 room frame single| $4000 cash| Sold by Title Guaranty and Trust Company, all in good "rentable" condition due to minor reconditioning. These had been renting from $30 to $40.| (
) |556 Targee Street| 6 room frame single| $3000 cash| Sold by Title Guaranty and Trust Company, all in good "rentable" condition due to minor reconditioning. These had been renting from $30 to $40.| (/n)

Principal thoroughfares: Clove Rd., Forest Ave., Broadway Quite a number of foreclosures in this area due to the fact that people here generally bought beyond their means. However, the area should develop rapidly with any economic improvement, due to its favorable location. Sales Prices (Dec.1940): Differ very little from 1938-9 levels. New building activity has supported a demand which has been reflected in institutional and HOLC sales. (HOLC at present owns 13, has sold 19). Title Guaranty and Trust Co. recently sold 5; Staten Island Savings Bank sold 1; Staten Island National Bank and Trust Co. sold 1; Northfield Savings and Loan Assn sold 1. Details not available on thesesales but prices ranged between $5500 and $8000. Title Guaranty and Trust Company sales have generally been somewhat below market and in sections where they had large holdings the effect of their activity has been felt. Rental Prices (Dec. 1940): About the same as 1938-39 with good demand up to $55. Comparatively high percentage of occupancy in this area because of its favorable location. Effects of Defense Activity: There has been considerable activity in the ship yards along the north shore of Staten Island, resulting from defense contracts. However, as yet the real estate market has derived little or no apparent benefit from the increased employment. Many workers commute from Brooklyn and from adjacent New Jersey and as yet have shown little disposition to move to nearby quarters. General Comments: About 25 to 30 new one family homes built last year. These are brick bungalows, and two story houses, principally located in the south part of the area which is high and somewhat undeveloped in a few spots. Prices range from $6500 to $7500 and FHA financing is offered. 10% cash payment required. Some homes have been built to order for owners who have purchased lots from the builder. There is room for further development in this small part and some activity may be expected. Institutions are believed to hold but little property here now. Typical Sales: |Address| Type Property| Sales Price & Terms| Remarks| (
) |22 Colonial Court| 7-Rm fr-stucco| $7500-$700 down; least and option; 5% interest|Sold by Westerleigh B/L at $100 net loss after some reconditioning| (
) |727 Bement Street| 6-Rm fr single| $5500-$500 down; lease and option; 5% interest| Sold by Northfield B/L Assn. No reconditioning| (
) |745 Pelton St.| 2 yr. old brk bungalow, 5 rms; hw heat; oil burner| $8000-$1500 down; $8 mo. per M, 5 1/2% int.| Sold "as is".| (
) |69 Clove Lakes Pl.| 8 rm. brk single, hw heat, good condition.| $6500-$1000 down; S&L mtg; 5 1/2% int.| A sacrifice sale by private owner.| (
) |20 Nutley Pl.| 6 rm.single; 2 car gar, hw heat| $4500-$500 down $8 mo. per M., Int. 5 1/2%.| In good condition, no reconditioning| (
) |725 Bement Ave.| 6 rm. fr single; hw hear; lot 40x100 garage| $4500-$500 down; $8 mo. per M. 5% int.| Completely recond., but sold at a loss.| (
